From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Shayne Legault <8741557+shaynelegault@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 16:20:08 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] mtd: apple-h2fmi: Enable P105 normal-block erase

Enable the provider callback for the P105 controller variant with the
measured 64 GB geometry.

The P105 trial erased all 16 lanes of block 122 and read every normal and
SLC page blank. Linux persisted the incremented wear, reused one page
through native accounting, and recovered the mapping on a fresh load. iOS
then moved the witness and reclaimed the block again. A later Linux boot
recovered wear 9 and scanned the block blank. Both iOS returns passed
System and Data filesystem checks, and the 64 MiB Linux witness retained
its SHA-256.

The existing SFTL path checks full ownership and publishes native
accounting before reuse. It reads each lane back after erase, then reloads
the medium before granting the block.

Assisted-by: LLM
---
 .../driver-api/mtd/apple-sftl-reclamation.rst | 30 ++++++++++++++-----
 drivers/mtd/Kconfig                           |  5 ++--
 drivers/mtd/devices/Kconfig                   |  5 ++--
 drivers/mtd/devices/mtd-apple-h2fmi.c         | 11 +++++--
 4 files changed, 36 insertions(+), 15 deletions(-)

diff --git a/Documentation/driver-api/mtd/apple-sftl-reclamation.rst b/Documentation/driver-api/mtd/apple-sftl-reclamation.rst
index 079a7585a..7ec40331a 100644
--- a/Documentation/driver-api/mtd/apple-sftl-reclamation.rst
+++ b/Documentation/driver-api/mtd/apple-sftl-reclamation.rst
@@ -11,8 +11,10 @@ The block writer allocates from verified blank blocks. An optional, verified
 ``erase_block`` provider operation lets the allocator also reclaim obsolete
 blocks. With a complete native accounting policy the allocator can also
 reserve fresh context sites, relocate live data and publish a checkpoint.
-P105 H2FMI does not enable physical erase or supply this policy. When verified
-blank blocks run out on that profile, a write returns ``-ENOSPC``.
+The measured 64 GB P105 enables normal-view erase and supplies this policy.
+The callback is scoped through the P105-specific controller compatible and
+the matching package geometry. A writer without that callback returns
+``-ENOSPC`` when blank blocks run out.
 
 Reclaiming an obsolete block
 ----------------------------
@@ -179,12 +181,24 @@ bit count. The 13G36 provider builder at 0x8109de94 also supplies CAU and view
 fields. Both saved parameter copies agree: page bits 7, block bits 12 and CAU
 bits 3. A normal block's page-0 row is ``(cau << 19) | (block << 7)``; the SLC
 view adds bit 22. The normal view reports 128 pages per block. Physical erase
-effects, the SLC alias relationship, timeout, retirement and partial completion
-across CAUs remain unqualified. The H2FMI implementation issues the
-single-row command and checks the erased status, data and OOB on all 128
-normal-view pages. Enabling its callback on hardware requires a qualified
-physical erase extent, exclusive ownership across all affected views, and
-device recovery and interruption checks.
+effects and the SLC alias relationship were checked on a 64 GB P105. The trial
+erased all 16 lanes of an obsolete block. Every one of its 2,048 normal pages
+and 1,024 SLC aliases then read erased, including data and OOB. Linux published
+the incremented wear before it reused the block. A fresh load recovered the
+same mapping and data.
+
+After iOS booted, its System and Data filesystem checks passed. iOS moved the
+test page, reclaimed the block again and saved its next wear value. A second
+Linux boot recovered that state and scanned the full block blank. The final
+iOS boot passed both filesystem checks and reread the 64 MiB Linux witness
+with the same SHA-256.
+
+The H2FMI implementation issues the single-row command and checks the erased
+status, data and OOB on all 128 normal-view pages. SFTL owns the complete
+16-lane extent and stops the writer after an uncertain result. Synthetic
+tests cover timeout, retirement and interrupted multi-lane erase behavior.
+The physical callback is enabled only for the P105 controller variant with
+the measured 64 GB geometry.
 
 The source research began with `Cascadia's NAND notes
 <https://github.com/rzxvx/Project-Cascadia/blob/c20b85ebb951c704d7e3a664c378dde8276e3928/docs/research/p105-nand.md>`_.
diff --git a/drivers/mtd/Kconfig b/drivers/mtd/Kconfig
index 7bebb81ee..c7311067a 100644
--- a/drivers/mtd/Kconfig
+++ b/drivers/mtd/Kconfig
@@ -220,8 +220,9 @@ config MTD_APPLE_SFTL
 
 	  Writes are appended to verified blank blocks. A provider with a
 	  qualified erase operation can also recycle verified retired blocks.
-	  The physical P105 provider remains append-only and returns ENOSPC
-	  when its supply of blank blocks runs out.
+	  The measured 64 GB P105 supplies this operation. Providers without
+	  a qualified erase operation return ENOSPC when their supply of blank
+	  blocks runs out.
 
 config MTD_APPLE_SFTL_KUNIT_TEST
 	tristate "KUnit tests for Apple VFL/SwissPPNFTL" if !KUNIT_ALL_TESTS
diff --git a/drivers/mtd/devices/Kconfig b/drivers/mtd/devices/Kconfig
index ef6a7facd..e6b5b22ad 100644
--- a/drivers/mtd/devices/Kconfig
+++ b/drivers/mtd/devices/Kconfig
@@ -219,8 +219,9 @@ config MTD_APPLE_H2FMI
 
 	  Pages are programmed whole, only when they read back erased, and
 	  each program is verified by reading it back. The aggregate PPN
-	  provider can verify a single-block erase on qualified hardware.
-	  Erase is disabled on the P105.
+	  provider verifies single-block erase on the measured 64 GB P105.
+	  The callback stays scoped to the P105 controller compatible and its
+	  matching package geometry.
 
 	  If unsure, say N.
 
diff --git a/drivers/mtd/devices/mtd-apple-h2fmi.c b/drivers/mtd/devices/mtd-apple-h2fmi.c
index b24d13e90..7f5e0eab7 100644
--- a/drivers/mtd/devices/mtd-apple-h2fmi.c
+++ b/drivers/mtd/devices/mtd-apple-h2fmi.c
@@ -1312,7 +1312,8 @@ static int h2fmi_register_sftl(struct h2fmi *fmi)
 		return 0;
 	fmi->ppn_ops.read_page = h2fmi_ppn_read;
 	fmi->ppn_ops.program_page = h2fmi_ppn_program;
-	if (variant && variant->normal_erase_qualified)
+	if (variant && variant->normal_erase_qualified && g->caus == 8 &&
+	    g->blocks == 2088 && g->pages == 128 && g->slc_pages == 64)
 		fmi->ppn_ops.erase_block = h2fmi_ppn_erase;
 	fmi->sftl = platform_device_register_data(fmi->dev, "apple-sftl",
 						 PLATFORM_DEVID_AUTO,
@@ -1607,12 +1608,16 @@ static void h2fmi_remove(struct platform_device *pdev)
 		mtd_device_unregister(&fmi->mtd[i]);
 }
 
-/* Normal/SLC aliasing and the physical erase extent remain unqualified. */
+/* The P105 compatible selects the qualified 64 GB normal-view extent. */
+static const struct h2fmi_variant p105ap_ppn = {
+	.normal_erase_qualified = true,
+};
+
 static const struct h2fmi_variant s5l8940x_ppn = {
-	.normal_erase_qualified = false,
 };
 
 static const struct of_device_id h2fmi_of_match[] = {
+	{ .compatible = "apple,p105ap-fmi", .data = &p105ap_ppn },
 	{ .compatible = "apple,s5l8940x-fmi", .data = &s5l8940x_ppn },
 	{ }
 };
